@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:architecture;desktop] mila-linux-single-instance
The application MUST use one stable GApplication id and HANDLES_OPEN activation so a second
launch forwards every `overalls://` URI to the existing process instead of creating another window.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security;navigation] mila-linux-links-navigate-only
An accepted `overalls://` URI MUST select a typed navigation target only; activation MUST NOT issue a
runtime command, change a deployment, approve a session, or add credentials.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security;protocol] mila-linux-route-closed-table
URI parsing MUST enforce the bounded closed route table and reject malformed authorities, unknown
paths, fragments, userinfo, encoded path segments, and non-canonical identifiers.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security;architecture] mila-linux-typed-runtime-boundary
The native UI/runtime seam MUST exchange typed request and response values through a Rust trait;
raw pointers, credentials, authentication tokens, and terminal bytes MUST NOT appear in its API or
be logged by the scaffold.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security;terminal] linux-terminal-renderer-bounded
The Linux terminal renderer MUST accept only decoded display text, reject unsupported control
characters and oversized chunks, and retain at most its configured viewport; terminal bytes MUST
remain owned by the authenticated transport and MUST NOT be logged or exposed to GTK state.


@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security;navigation] linux-open-activation-batch
A desktop open activation MUST retain only the bounded set of parsed navigation routes; malformed
or excess URIs MUST become a count and MUST NOT be echoed, executed, or persisted.


@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security;desktop] linux-machine-view-bounded
Machine list and detail labels MUST be derived from the validated authenticated listing, reject
control characters before GTK display, and expose permissions only as presentation hints.


@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security;terminal] linux-terminal-transport-authenticated
The Linux PTY seam MUST require an authenticated session before attach, bind all operations to the
current generation, validate input and resize permissions, and keep PTY bytes outside GTK state.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security;web] linux-web-browser-fence
The GTK web action MUST launch only a Rust-validated public HTTPS endpoint whose
port matches the authenticated projection; private, stale, malformed, or
non-matching destinations MUST be refused before invoking the desktop browser.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:architecture;desktop] linux-gtk-views-thin
GTK code in `src/main.rs`, `src/views.rs` and `src/pages.rs` MUST NOT parse routes, choose request
identifiers, decode PTY bytes or server results, or decide deployment, terminal, SSH-key or
navigation state; it MUST delegate to the library modules (`deployments`, `terminal_surface`,
`terminal_text`, `ssh_keys_view`, `deep_link`, `route`, `porsche_results`, `adapter`'s
`RequestLedger`) so the headless journey driver exercises the same decisions. Every visible string
on the Machines and Threads pages MUST come from the shared catalogs.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security;desktop] linux-authority-drop-on-switch
Selecting a different deployment, removing the selected deployment, signing out or closing the
window MUST clear the authenticated session, sign-in generation, machine listing, terminal
generation and SSH-key rows before any later action can use them.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security;architecture] linux-gtk-signin-live-session
The GTK Sign in button MUST start the window's SignInDriver for the selected deployment's
resolved connection; the window MUST bind only sessions the driver establishes and MUST release
the bound session and every projection derived from it on loss or sign-out. The fixture approver
hook MUST be compiled only with the `gtk-journey` feature.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security;desktop] linux-page-action-loss
A page action that fails with a transport or stale-handle error MUST report the session lost to the
window's SignInDriver before it returns, releasing the session and every projection derived from it
and starting the next sign-in generation without waiting for the heartbeat.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:verification] linux-journey-backend-effects
Each lifecycle, sharing, web and thread step of both Linux journeys MUST assert its effect through an
authenticated read on the same session (`ls`, `show`, `share show`, `web show`, `request show`,
`thread show`, `thread turns`); a widget change alone MUST NOT pass a step. A check that finds the
session `Busy` (the heartbeat probe holds it) MUST poll again rather than fail.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security;desktop] linux-terminal-close-detach
The GTK terminal page's Close MUST ask for confirmation and then end the named server session through
`LiveSession::terminal_end` (Marshall `terminal kill`), closing this device's channel first. Detach
MUST close only this device's channel and viewport, leaving the server session and its programs
running. An empty session field makes Attach open or rejoin the account's own session and show its id.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:verification] linux-journey-guest-terminal
When the fixture descriptor names `seed.terminal_machine_id`, both Linux journeys MUST run only
terminal operations on that machine. They MUST open its session (retrying `temporarily_unavailable`
while the guest boots), see the shell-computed echo line, see `stty size` answer the resized
dimensions, see the earlier output replay on a fresh viewport after detach and a named reattach, and
end it with `terminal kill`. That step passes only after a named reattach is refused or its stream
ends. The guestless refusal check stays on the seeded machine, which is never observed running.
